Yes. What you’re describing maps well onto state-dependent cognition and mood-congruent retrieval: an emotional state changes what memories, interpretations, action policies, and associations become easiest to access.

So anger is not just an extra variable added to neutral thinking. It can partially reconfigure the search space.

S_{\text{anger}}
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{threat cues}\uparrow\\
\text{hostile interpretations}\uparrow\\
\text{confrontational actions}\uparrow\\
\text{matching memories}\uparrow\\
\text{alternative framings}\downarrow
\end{cases}

Then each angry interpretation generates more evidence for anger:

S \rightarrow \text{matching thought} \rightarrow \text{matching memory}
\rightarrow \text{matching interpretation} \rightarrow S^{+}

That is the trap. The state becomes partly self-retrieving.

And your hammer/nail description captures the computational failure:

\text{available operator} \rightarrow \text{everything encoded as its operand}

Anger has tremendous utility—boundary enforcement, energy, courage, rapid prioritization—but once it monopolizes representation, every ambiguity starts looking like insult, opposition, betrayal, or obstacle.

The goal therefore isn’t merely suppress the emotion.

It is to maintain operator plurality while inside the emotion.

That gives several different moves.

1. Work through the state

Use the energy without accepting its ontology.

Anger says:

Act.

You can preserve the action energy while changing the action:

\text{anger} \rightarrow \text{exercise}

\text{anger} \rightarrow \text{assertive boundary}

\text{anger} \rightarrow \text{focused work}

That’s conversion.

⸻

2. Change coordinates inside the same state

Don’t try to jump from furious to serene.

Move somewhere adjacent that anger can actually reach:

\text{anger}
\rightarrow
\text{determination}
\rightarrow
\text{agency}
\rightarrow
\text{problem solving}

That’s much easier than:

\text{rage} \rightarrow \text{peace}

You’re traversing the emotional manifold rather than demanding teleportation.

⸻

3. The knight’s move

This is the especially good one.

A knight escapes a blocked line because it does not travel along the obvious axis.

Psychologically:

If I cannot change the emotion directly, change a different variable that changes the emotion indirectly.

For example:

\text{attention target}
\rightarrow
\text{different interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{state shift}

or:

\text{body position}
\rightarrow
\text{breathing}
\rightarrow
\text{arousal}
\rightarrow
\text{cognition}

or:

\text{time horizon}
\rightarrow
\text{meaning}
\rightarrow
\text{emotion}

You flank the state through another subsystem.

That is a genuine control-theory move: when one actuator is saturated, control the system through another available channel.

⸻

4. Compartmentalization without amputation

This is subtler.

Sometimes you cannot afford to fully process an emotion at the moment.

So:

\text{emotion remains active}

but:

\text{emotion's control authority}\downarrow

You don’t need to pretend:

“I am not angry.”

You can say:

“Anger gets a seat, but not the wheel.”

Then preserve access to capabilities that anger normally suppresses:

* long-horizon thinking;
* empathy;
* humor;
* probabilistic reasoning;
* alternative hypothesis generation;
* procedural discipline;
* awareness of consequences.

That’s functional compartmentalization rather than dissociation from the state.

⸻

5. Carry tools across states

This may be the deepest part of your observation.

A calm mind can develop tools that the angry mind later inherits.

For example, while calm you create:

\text{trigger} \rightarrow \text{three candidate interpretations}

Then when angry you don’t have to invent cognitive flexibility. You execute the cached procedure.

That’s effectively:

\text{State A constructs tool}
\rightarrow
\text{tool stored}
\rightarrow
\text{State B invokes tool}

A capability becomes state-portable.

And you can train this recursively: practice recalling calm-state tools under progressively stronger arousal until the tool remains available across a wider part of state space.

So the architecture becomes:

\boxed{
\text{state}
\rightarrow
\text{recognize state}
\rightarrow
\text{identify its native tools}
\rightarrow
\text{preserve useful energy}
\rightarrow
\text{import missing operators}
\rightarrow
\text{flank if direct control fails}
}

The aim isn’t emotional neutrality.

It is emotional interoperability.

Anger remains capable of anger’s job without becoming your entire cognitive operating system. Fear can detect danger without becoming the only map. Sadness can mark loss without defining the future. Desire can reveal attraction without automatically becoming command.

You keep the specialized subsystem—and retain access to the larger mind around it.